{% if error %}<p class="bad">{% error %}</p>{% end %}

<h2>Sign in</h2>

<p>No password: this demo is about what a key can do, and a password field
would be the one part of it nobody needed to read. Any address with an
<code>@</code> works, and the account is created on first sight as an
<code>admin</code>.</p>

<form method="post" action="{% url.login %}" class="row">
  {% raw csrf %}
  <input name="email" type="email" required autocomplete="email"
         placeholder="you@example.com" aria-label="email">
  <button>Sign in</button>
</form>

<h3>What this demonstrates</h3>

<p>A key is <strong>minted once</strong>. The plaintext is on the response
that made it and nowhere else - the row keeps a SHA-256 digest and a short
prefix, which is enough to recognise a key in a list and not enough to
rebuild one.</p>

<p><strong>Scopes are a flat set</strong> with a declared vocabulary:
<code>write</code> does not imply <code>read</code> unless the application
issues both, because an API scope is a permission and not a rank.</p>

<p><strong>A key answers to its owner's standing.</strong> Suspend the
account and every key stops; demote it and an <code>admin</code> scope drops
while the rest keep working. The key's own row does not change.</p>

<p><strong>Denial is an API's denial.</strong> 401 with
<code>WWW-Authenticate: Bearer</code> for a credential that is not good, 403
for a scope it lacks, and never a redirect - a browser is not what is on the
other end of a key.</p>

{% if users %}<h3>Accounts that exist</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>id</th><th>email</th><th>role</th><th>standing</th></tr>
{% for u in users %}  <tr>
    <td><code>{% u.id %}</code></td>
    <td>{% u.email %}</td>
    <td><code>{% u.role %}</code></td>
    <td>{% if u.suspended %}<b class="bad">suspended</b>{% else %}good{% end %}</td>
  </tr>
{% end %}</table>
{% end %}
